When `auxiliary_device_add()` fails, the error block calls `auxiliary_device_uninit()`, which frees the auxiliary device and sets `bd->auxr_dev = NULL`. Because there is no `return` statement, execution continues to `bd->auxr_dev->net = bd->netdev`, which dereferences the null pointer, causing a use‑after‑free/null‑pointer dereference bug.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (rc) {
    dev_warn(bd->dev, "Failed to add auxiliary device for ROCE\n");
    auxiliary_device_uninit(aux_dev);
    bd->flags &= ~BNGE_EN_ROCE;
    return;
}
Reason:
Adding `return;` ensures that after cleaning up (uninit and flag clearing) the function exits immediately, preventing the subsequent null dereference on `bd->auxr_dev`. This matches the auxiliary bus documentation requirement to return after uninit on failure.

